# Independently authored PG ARRAY multi-dim shape-
# tracking CORRUPTION-class sentinel slice for the GPL
# MTR-format runner. NOT copied or adapted from MySQL's
# mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (read carefully — DO NOT duplicate):
#   - 1D `cardinality(ARRAY[1,2,3])` and
#     `cardinality(ARRAY[]::INT[])`: pinned at
#     array_type_basic_extra.test:135-138 / .result:80-86.
#     ONLY 1D shapes. The multi-dim shape (where the bug
#     surfaces) is genuinely ZERO prior.
#   - 1D `array_length(arr, 1)` shapes: pinned across
#     array_type_basic_extra (via `ARRAY_LENGTH(...,1)`
#     idiom at lines 2-12) and array_subscript_anyall_
#     basic_extra. NO prior coverage of `array_length(arr,
#     dim>1)` on a multi-dim literal — the shape that
#     would reveal the missing inner-axis tracking.
#   - 1D `array_dims(ARRAY[1,2,3])` and
#     `array_ndims(ARRAY[1,2,3])`: pinned as "unknown
#     function" at array_subscript_anyall_basic_extra.
#     test:115-117 / .result:21-25. The 1D shape ERRORS
#     at name lookup before arg evaluation, so multi-dim
#     versions of those two calls collapse to the
#     IDENTICAL "unknown function" fingerprint — NOT
#     re-pinned here (would be a same-fingerprint
#     duplicate per the gap-scan rule).
#   - `generate_subscripts(arr, dim)` set-returning
#     function: ZERO prior across `tests/main/*.test`.
#     This slice pins it.
#   - `= ANY(ARRAY[...])` match + no-match shapes:
#     INT-not-found (`9 = ANY(...)` → 0), TEXT-not-found
#     (`'z' = ANY(...)` → 0), NULL-handling, `= ALL`
#     match/no-match: ALL pinned at array_type_basic_
#     extra.test:80-90 and array_subscript_anyall_basic_
#     extra.test:92-95. A stale probe note suggested
#     `5 = ANY(ARRAY[1,2,3])` errored; re-probed via
#     mysqlwire — it returns `0` correctly, MATCHING the
#     `9 = ANY` pin already in place. NO new bug to
#     pin on the ANY path. (Per the don't-fabricate
#     rule: a stale finding that doesn't reproduce is
#     dropped, not pinned as fake.)
#
# Genuinely ZERO prior coverage on these shapes (grep
# verified across `tests/main/*.test` for `cardinality\(`,
# `array_length\(.*, *[2-9]\)`, `generate_subscripts`):
#   - `cardinality(arr)` on a multi-dim literal (2D, 3D).
#   - `array_length(multi_dim, 2+)` — querying the size
#     of any axis OTHER than dim=1.
#   - `array_upper(multi_dim, 2+)` / `array_lower(multi_
#     dim, 2+)` — same axis-shape introspection family.
#   - `generate_subscripts(arr, dim)` in EITHER FROM-
#     clause (SRF) or SELECT-scalar position.
#
# Pre-existing pattern fingerprints this slice joins:
#   - CORRUPTION-class family (silent wrong result, no
#     error, no warning, output structurally a valid
#     INTEGER): PR #421 (DEFAULT subquery stored as text
#     literal), PR #432 (TIMESTAMP(n) precision silent-
#     drop), PR #438 (EXPLAIN option modifiers FORMAT/
#     ANALYZE/BUFFERS silent-drop), PR #440 (PG printf-
#     style `format()` silently re-routed to MySQL
#     numeric FORMAT), PR #441 (`to_char()` partial-
#     impl format codes kept LITERALLY). Shared
#     mechanism: the wire reports success, the value
#     LOOKS LIKE a plausible answer, but the content is
#     wrong against PG semantics. Particularly hard to
#     catch in eyeballed test runs because the 1D case
#     accidentally produces the right number.
#   - Two-fingerprint SRF-vs-scalar dispatch family:
#     PR #423 (`pg_get_keywords()`) and PR #435 (MySQL
#     @var/@@sysvar SET-vs-SELECT paths). `generate_
#     subscripts` joins this family: in FROM position
#     the SRF dispatcher errors UPPER-cased
#     ("GENERATE_SUBSCRIPTS"); in SELECT scalar
#     position the regular scalar dispatcher errors
#     lower-cased ("generate_subscripts"). Two error
#     paths, two normalization conventions, one
#     missing implementation.
#
# THE ROOT-CAUSE BUG (CORRUPTION class):
#
#   PG arrays preserve full multi-dimensional shape:
#   `ARRAY[[1,2],[3,4]]` is a 2x2 array of INT with
#   `array_dims` = `[1:2][1:2]`, `array_ndims` = 2,
#   `cardinality` = 4 (TOTAL element count across all
#   dimensions), `array_length(arr, 1)` = 2,
#   `array_length(arr, 2)` = 2.
#
#   In zeta, multi-dim ARRAY literals appear to parse
#   and store as nested-array values (the
#   `pg_named_window_multidim_array_support_sentinel`
#   slice pins that the constructor accepts them and
#   the wire renders the nested Debug repr), BUT the
#   axis-shape metadata is NOT tracked as PG semantics
#   would require:
#
#     (a) `cardinality()` returns the OUTER-dimension
#         length only — NOT the total element count.
#         For `ARRAY[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6]]`: PG → 6, zeta
#         → 3. The 1D case (`cardinality(ARRAY[1,2,3])`
#         = 3) accidentally produces the right number
#         because total-elements == outer-dim-length
#         when ndims=1.
#
#     (b) `array_length(arr, dim)` returns NULL for
#         dim>1 regardless of whether the input is
#         actually multi-dim. The 1D output `NULL` for
#         `array_length(ARRAY[1,2,3], 2)` matches PG
#         (a 1D array has no second axis). The 2D
#         output `NULL` for `array_length(ARRAY[[1,2],
#         [3,4]], 2)` DOES NOT match PG (PG → 2). One
#         shape-fingerprint, two semantic meanings —
#         the OK-on-1D pin masks the WRONG-on-2D bug.
#         Same root cause: the inner-axis metadata is
#         absent from the stored value.
#
#     (c) `array_upper(arr, 2)` / `array_lower(arr, 2)`
#         on a 2D literal exhibit the same NULL-on-
#         dim>1 behavior. Same root cause.
#
#   The shape-tracking gap is uniform: a multi-dim
#   literal is stored as a nested Array Value with
#   element-type Array(T), but the introspection
#   functions only inspect the outer Vec length. Inner
#   axis sizes are never computed or stored. The bug
#   class is CORRUPTION (silent-wrong-result, not gap
#   error) because the OUTPUT TYPE is INTEGER (the same
#   type PG returns), so downstream type-aware code
#   accepts it without complaint.
#
#   Apps affected: any port from PG that uses
#   `cardinality(multi_dim)` to compute total element
#   counts (very common in array-storage analytics);
#   any code that uses `array_length(arr, 2)` /
#   `array_upper(arr, 2)` to iterate the inner axis
#   of a 2D matrix/grid. Silent wrong counts get
#   propagated into report templates, loop bounds,
#   buffer-allocation sizes — anywhere the 1D pin
#   happened to "look right" in eyeballed testing.
#
# THE INTROSPECTION GAP (UNKNOWN-FN class):
#
#   `generate_subscripts(arr, dim)` is the PG set-
#   returning function that yields each valid
#   subscript value for the given axis. It is the
#   canonical PG idiom for iterating an array with
#   the index in scope:
#
#     SELECT i, arr[i] FROM generate_subscripts(arr, 1) g(i);
#
#   In zeta it dispatches through two distinct error
#   paths depending on call-site position:
#
#     (i) FROM-clause SRF call:
#         `FROM generate_subscripts(ARRAY['a','b','c'], 1)`
#         → "plan error: unknown set-returning function:
#         GENERATE_SUBSCRIPTS" (UPPER-cased — the SRF
#         dispatcher normalizes the name).
#
#     (ii) SELECT-scalar call:
#         `SELECT generate_subscripts(ARRAY[10,20,30], 1)`
#         → "execution error: unknown function:
#         generate_subscripts" (lower-cased — the scalar
#         dispatcher preserves the source casing).
#
#   Two error paths, two normalization conventions,
#   one missing implementation. Same two-fingerprint
#   dispatch witness as PR #423 (`pg_get_keywords`)
#   and PR #435 (MySQL `@var`/`@@sysvar`).
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: BUG — `cardinality(multi_dim)` returns
#     OUTER-dim length, not total element count. The 1D
#     case (`cardinality(ARRAY[1,2,3])` = 3) accidentally
#     works because outer-dim == total when ndims=1.
#     Substitute (until fixed): for 2D arrays, compute
#     `array_length(arr,1) * array_length(arr,2)` —
#     except this ALSO fails (see next note). The only
#     reliable substitute is to track total counts
#     out-of-band at insertion time.
#   # zeta-adapt: BUG — `array_length(multi_dim, dim>=2)`
#     returns NULL regardless of whether the inner axis
#     exists. Same root cause: inner-axis metadata
#     absent. No reliable substitute in pure SQL;
#     iterate the OUTER axis and treat each row's inner
#     array as a separate 1D array
#     (`array_length(arr[i], 1)`).
#   # zeta-adapt: BUG — `array_upper(multi_dim, dim>=2)`
#     / `array_lower(multi_dim, dim>=2)` exhibit the same
#     NULL-on-inner-axis behavior. Same substitute as
#     `array_length`.
#   # zeta-adapt: GAP — `generate_subscripts(arr, dim)`
#     unimplemented in both FROM and SELECT positions.
#     Substitute: `FROM generate_series(1, array_length(
#     arr, 1)) g(i)` — gives 1-based subscripts for the
#     OUTER axis; combine with `arr[i]` for value
#     access. Note: this substitute uses
#     `array_length(arr, 1)` which IS correct for the
#     outer axis (the bug only affects dim>=2).
#
# Wire divergence check: probed both wires. Both
# `cardinality()` and `array_length()` are scalar
# functions resolved in the planner (wire-independent);
# both wires return identical numeric output (and
# identical wrong output). The `generate_subscripts`
# dispatch error originates in the planner SRF
# resolver and scalar resolver, also wire-independent.
# Pinning on the mysqlwire path is sufficient.

--echo === CONTROL — cardinality 1D works (accidentally — outer-dim == total when ndims=1) ===

# Pinned at array_type_basic_extra.test:137 already.
# Re-asserted here as the IMMEDIATE control adjacent
# to the multi-dim BUG pin, so the regression diff
# shows "1D OK, 2D wrong" side-by-side on a single
# screen.
SELECT cardinality(ARRAY[1, 2, 3]) AS c_1d;

--echo === BUG #1 (CORRUPTION) — cardinality 2D returns OUTER-dim count, not total elements ===

# PG: 6 (3 rows * 2 cols = 6 total elements).
# zeta: 3 (outer-dim length only — the inner [1,2]
# / [3,4] / [5,6] sub-arrays are treated as opaque
# elements). Silent wrong result; output type is
# INTEGER, downstream type-aware code accepts it.
SELECT cardinality(ARRAY[[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]) AS c_2d_3x2;

# PG: 6 (2 rows * 3 cols = 6).
# zeta: 2 (outer-dim length only).
# Confirms the bug is "outer-dim length" not "first
# inner length" — the orientation swap (2x3 vs 3x2)
# changes the wrong answer in the predictable way.
SELECT cardinality(ARRAY[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]) AS c_2d_2x3;

# PG: 8 (2 * 2 * 2 = 8 total).
# zeta: 2 (outermost length only). Confirms the bug
# scales to higher dimensions — only the outermost
# Vec length is read.
SELECT cardinality(ARRAY[[[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[5, 6], [7, 8]]]) AS c_3d_2x2x2;

--echo === CONTROL — array_length 1D works (and dim=2 correctly returns NULL) ===

# 1D, axis=1: 5. CORRECT (matches PG).
SELECT array_length(ARRAY[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 1) AS al_1d_dim1;

# 1D, axis=2: NULL. CORRECT (matches PG — a 1D array
# has no second axis). Same observable as the 2D-dim-2
# BUG below, but here it is the CORRECT answer. The
# bug only surfaces when the input HAS a second axis
# yet zeta still returns NULL — see next block.
SELECT array_length(ARRAY[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2) AS al_1d_dim2_correct_null;

--echo === BUG #2 (CORRUPTION, same root cause) — array_length(multi_dim, dim>=2) returns NULL despite inner axis existing ===

# 2D, axis=1: 2 (outer-dim length). CORRECT.
# 2D, axis=2: NULL. WRONG — PG returns 2 (each row
# has 2 elements). zeta does not track inner-axis
# metadata, so dim>=2 collapses to NULL regardless of
# whether the inner axis actually exists.
SELECT array_length(ARRAY[[1, 2], [3, 4]], 1) AS al_2d_dim1_ok,
       array_length(ARRAY[[1, 2], [3, 4]], 2) AS al_2d_dim2_wrong_null;

# 2D, axis=2 on a 2x3 shape: zeta NULL, PG 3.
# Same fingerprint, different inner-axis size to
# confirm the bug is not "always returns NULL when
# dim=2 even if true axis size happens to be NULL".
SELECT array_length(ARRAY[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], 2) AS al_2d_dim2_wrong_null_3;

# array_upper / array_lower on dim=2 exhibit the
# same NULL-on-inner-axis behavior — confirming the
# root cause is shared across the axis-introspection
# family (one shape-tracking gap surfaces in three
# different functions).
SELECT array_upper(ARRAY[[1, 2], [3, 4]], 2) AS au_2d_dim2_wrong_null,
       array_lower(ARRAY[[1, 2], [3, 4]], 2) AS al_2d_dim2_lower_wrong_null;

--echo === GAP (two-fingerprint) — generate_subscripts(arr, dim) ===

# FROM-clause SRF: planner SRF dispatcher errors with
# the name UPPER-cased ("GENERATE_SUBSCRIPTS"). Same
# normalization convention as the
# pg_create_function_non_trigger_sentinel SRF pin
# ("SRF") and PR #423 (pg_get_keywords).
--error 1105
SELECT * FROM generate_subscripts(ARRAY['a', 'b', 'c'], 1) AS g(i);

# SELECT scalar position: scalar dispatcher errors
# with the name LOWER-cased ("generate_subscripts").
# Different error path, different normalization
# convention, same missing implementation. Two-
# fingerprint pattern witness (PR #423, PR #435).
--error 1105
SELECT generate_subscripts(ARRAY[10, 20, 30], 1) AS s;
